WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:00.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the agonizing wait for the body of a  loved one  this is the family of ihab al-wazini a  well-known organizer in iraq's  intergovernment protest movement  he was shot dead the night before just  outside his home in iraq's southern city  of karbala  ihab's mother told al-jazeera he had  received numerous threats from  paramilitary groups backed by iran  the militias are everywhere in the  streets they have a list which includes  the names of all the activists  they will kill them one by one today  they killed my son  and soon they'll kill another the  funeral procession arrived in the  scorching afternoon heat  their religious oaks punctuated with  desperate cries for survival  the parties are killing us scream the  men who once stood by ihab's side in  kerball's protest squares demanding  change  but is anyone listening inside ehab's  family said their final goodbye  their anger directed at both the killers  and the government  who failed to protect him we don't trust  the government  and we don't trust this political  process if there's to be justice  there should be an international  investigation ehab is one of 34  activists assassinated since protests  first began in october 2019  according to iraq's human rights  commission a third were killed after a  new government was formed in may 2020  promising to end impunity when prime  minister mustafa al-qaeda me came to  power almost a year ago  he promised to reign in armed groups  that acted outside of the state's  control  and to bring those responsible for the  deaths of protesters and activists to  account  but only a handful of arrests have been  made and no one  has been convicted and so the killings  and the funerals go  on the protesters drove ihab's body to  catabola central square  once packed with crowds rallying for a  cause  but after the movement crumbled amid  brutal repression  they mostly just gathered to mourn those  they lost  few here dare to speak on camera in fear  they might be next  some expect violence to further flare  ahead of october elections  these assassinations don't come out of  nowhere they are organized  it's linked to the elections that have  been set for next october and all these  politicians are scared  and think that october protesters will  compete for their power to bring about  change  that's why they're targeting the  activist and today he had paid the price  the prime minister promised justice and  ordered an  inquiry but none of these investigations  have borne fruit in the past  leaving little hope for accountability  in the future  simona faltin al-jazeera kerbala

